# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Line endings, decided by the repository rather than by whoever cloned it.
#
#  THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES, which is worth understanding because it wastes a
#  genuinely surprising amount of time:
#
#  Git has a setting called `core.autocrlf`. On Windows it is commonly `true`,
#  which means "store LF in the repository, but convert to CRLF in my working
#  directory on checkout." It is a per-machine setting, so it is not shared, and
#  every contributor may have it set differently.
#
#  That produces two failures that look unrelated to their cause:
#
#    • Prettier is configured here with `endOfLine: lf`. After a checkout on a
#      machine with autocrlf=true, every file on disk has CRLF endings, so
#      `npm run format:check` fails on all of them — while the identical commit
#      passes in Linux CI. The report is "my formatter is broken", the cause is a
#      git setting nobody looked at.
#
#    • Someone with a different setting touches one line of a file and the diff
#      shows all 400 lines changed, because the endings were rewritten too. The
#      real change becomes unreviewable.
#
#  `text=auto eol=lf` tells git: treat these as text, normalise to LF on commit,
#  AND check them out as LF regardless of what core.autocrlf says locally. The
#  bytes on disk now match the bytes in the repository on every platform, so
#  Prettier, ESLint and CI all agree.
#
#  WHY IN THE REPO RATHER THAN A GIT SETTING: .gitattributes is committed, so it
#  applies to everyone who clones — including CI and any future contributor. A
#  `git config` fix works on exactly one machine and has to be remembered again on
#  the next one. Configuration that must hold for the project belongs in the
#  project.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary, so git never attempts line-ending conversion on them.
# Corrupting a PNG by rewriting bytes that happened to look like a CRLF is a real
# thing that happens when this is left off.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
